      "content": "<h3>What is an ArrayList?</h3>\n<p>An <strong>ArrayList</strong> is a <strong>resizable array</strong>. A normal Java array has a fixed size that you choose once; an ArrayList starts small and <strong>grows by itself</strong> when you add more elements, and shrinks when you remove them. Think of a list of forts you want to visit: you can add a new fort at the end, insert one in the middle, or cross one off, without ever deciding the size in advance.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It lives in the <code>java.util</code> package and implements the <code>List</code> interface (so it is an <strong>ordered</strong> collection).</li>\n<li><strong>Order is kept:</strong> elements stay in the order you added them, and every element has an <strong>index</strong> starting at <code>0</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Duplicates are allowed:</strong> the same value can appear many times.</li>\n<li><strong>null is allowed:</strong> you can store <code>null</code> (but avoid it in real programs).</li>\n<li><strong>Fast reading:</strong> <code>get(index)</code> is instant, because inside it is an array.</li>\n<li><strong>Not thread-safe:</strong> if several threads change one ArrayList at the same time, wrap it or use a concurrent list (see the Expert tab).</li>\n<li>It stores <strong>objects only</strong>. For numbers use the wrapper types: <code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code>, not <code>ArrayList&lt;int&gt;</code>.</li>\n</ul>"
