      "content": "<h3>Creating an ArrayList: the three constructors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;()</code>: an empty list. Internally it starts with <strong>no array at all</strong> and allocates room for <strong>10</strong> elements on the first <code>add</code>.</li>\n<li><code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(int initialCapacity)</code>: an empty list that already has room for that many elements. Use it when you know roughly how many items are coming; it avoids repeated growing.</li>\n<li><code>new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(Collection c)</code>: a <strong>copy</strong> of another collection, in that collection's iteration order.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> declare the variable with the <strong>interface</strong> type, <code>List&lt;String&gt; forts = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();</code>. Your code then does not care which list class is behind it, and you can switch to another implementation later by changing one word.</p>",
