      "content": "<h3>2. add(int index, E e)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>void add(int index, E element)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> nothing (void)</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(n): later elements shift right</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Inserts the element <strong>at the position you choose</strong>. The element that was there, and everything after it, moves one step to the right. Valid indexes are <code>0</code> to <code>size()</code> (adding at <code>size()</code> is the same as adding at the end).</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Uses <code>System.arraycopy</code> to shift the tail, so inserting near the front of a big list is expensive. If you insert at the front very often, an <code>ArrayDeque</code> or <code>LinkedList</code> may suit better.</p>\n<p><strong>Watch out:</strong> Index <code>size() + 1</code> or more throws <code>IndexOutOfBoundsException</code>; you cannot leave gaps in a list.</p>",
