    {
      "content": "<h3>Practice question 1: Trek booking desk (add, search, update, cancel, sort, page)</h3>\n<p><strong>Level:</strong> Medium</p>\n<p><strong>Problem:</strong> A trek company keeps today's bookings in an <code>ArrayList</code>. This is the everyday life of a list: bookings arrive, a customer changes the fort, somebody cancels, and the desk prints a sorted page of results.</p>\n<p><strong>Your task:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>add</strong> five bookings and <strong>add(index, e)</strong> a VIP at the top.</li>\n<li><strong>indexOf / get</strong> to find a booking, <strong>set</strong> to move it to another fort.</li>\n<li><strong>removeIf</strong> to cancel every booking for one fort.</li>\n<li><strong>sort</strong> by fort, then print the list in <strong>pages</strong> with <strong>subList</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Try it on your own first. The program below is one complete solution, with the real output it prints.</em></p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// Real-time practice: a trek booking desk built on ArrayList\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class TrekBookingDesk {\n    record Booking(String customerCity, String fort, int people) { }\n\n    static void print(String title, List<Booking> list) {\n        System.out.println(\"--- \" + title + \" (\" + list.size() + \") ---\");\n        for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {\n            Booking b = list.get(i);\n            System.out.println((i + 1) + \". \" + b.fort() + \" | \" + b.customerCity() + \" | \" + b.people() + \" people\");\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Booking> bookings = new ArrayList<>();\n\n        // 1. New bookings arrive one by one\n        bookings.add(new Booking(\"Pune\", \"Raigad\", 4));\n        bookings.add(new Booking(\"Mumbai\", \"Torna\", 2));\n        bookings.add(new Booking(\"Nashik\", \"Lohagad\", 6));\n        bookings.add(new Booking(\"Nagpur\", \"Raigad\", 3));\n        bookings.add(new Booking(\"Kolhapur\", \"Panhala\", 5));\n        bookings.add(0, new Booking(\"Delhi\", \"Sinhagad\", 8));            // a VIP group goes to the top\n        print(\"After booking\", bookings);\n\n        // 2. Find a booking and change its fort\n        int position = -1;\n        for (int i = 0; i < bookings.size(); i++) {\n            if (bookings.get(i).customerCity().equals(\"Mumbai\")) {\n                position = i;\n            }\n        }\n        Booking old = bookings.get(position);\n        bookings.set(position, new Booking(old.customerCity(), \"Purandar\", old.people()));     // customer changed the fort\n        System.out.println(\"Mumbai booking moved to \" + bookings.get(position).fort());\n\n        // 3. Cancel every Raigad booking\n        boolean cancelled = bookings.removeIf(b -> b.fort().equals(\"Raigad\"));\n        System.out.println(\"Raigad cancelled: \" + cancelled);\n\n        // 4. Sort by fort name, then by group size (largest first)\n        bookings.sort(Comparator.comparing(Booking::fort).thenComparing(Comparator.comparingInt(Booking::people).reversed()));\n        print(\"Sorted\", bookings);\n\n        // 5. Show the list two bookings per page\n        int pageSize = 2;\n        for (int from = 0, page = 1; from < bookings.size(); from += pageSize, page++) {\n            int to = Math.min(from + pageSize, bookings.size());\n            System.out.println(\"Page \" + page + \": \" + bookings.subList(from, to).stream().map(Booking::fort).toList());\n        }\n\n        int total = bookings.stream().mapToInt(Booking::people).sum();\n        System.out.println(\"Total trekkers today: \" + total);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// --- After booking (6) ---\n// 1. Sinhagad | Delhi | 8 people\n// 2. Raigad | Pune | 4 people\n// 3. Torna | Mumbai | 2 people\n// 4. Lohagad | Nashik | 6 people\n// 5. Raigad | Nagpur | 3 people\n// 6. Panhala | Kolhapur | 5 people\n// Mumbai booking moved to Purandar\n// Raigad cancelled: true\n// --- Sorted (4) ---\n// 1. Lohagad | Nashik | 6 people\n// 2. Panhala | Kolhapur | 5 people\n// 3. Purandar | Mumbai | 2 people\n// 4. Sinhagad | Delhi | 8 people\n// Page 1: [Lohagad, Panhala]\n// Page 2: [Purandar, Sinhagad]\n// Total trekkers today: 21"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Practice question 2: Fort visitor log (filter, group, statistics)</h3>\n<p><strong>Level:</strong> Medium</p>\n<p><strong>Problem:</strong> A fort office exports a month of visitor records into an <code>ArrayList</code> and answers management questions: who came from which city, which day was busiest, and what is the average group.</p>\n<p><strong>Your task:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Build the list from raw data and <strong>copy</strong> it so the original is not changed.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>streams on the list</strong> to filter, group and count.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Collections.max</strong> and <strong>frequency</strong> for quick answers.</li>\n<li>Remove duplicates while keeping order with a <strong>LinkedHashSet</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>Try it on your own first. The program below is one complete solution, with the real output it prints.</em></p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// Real-time practice: analysing a visitor log stored in an ArrayList\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.Collections;\nimport java.util.LinkedHashSet;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\nimport java.util.stream.Collectors;\n\npublic class VisitorLogAnalysis {\n    record Visit(String city, int day, int people) { }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Visit> log = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\n            new Visit(\"Pune\", 1, 4), new Visit(\"Mumbai\", 1, 2), new Visit(\"Pune\", 2, 6),\n            new Visit(\"Nashik\", 2, 3), new Visit(\"Pune\", 3, 5), new Visit(\"Nagpur\", 3, 1),\n            new Visit(\"Mumbai\", 3, 7), new Visit(\"Pune\", 4, 2)));\n\n        // Who came from Pune?\n        List<Visit> fromPune = log.stream().filter(v -> v.city().equals(\"Pune\")).toList();\n        System.out.println(\"Visits from Pune : \" + fromPune.size());\n\n        // People per day (TreeMap keeps the days in order)\n        Map<Integer, Integer> perDay = new TreeMap<>();\n        for (Visit v : log) {\n            perDay.merge(v.day(), v.people(), Integer::sum);\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"People per day   : \" + perDay);\n\n        // Cities in the order they first appeared, without repeats\n        List<String> cities = new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(log.stream().map(Visit::city).toList()));\n        System.out.println(\"Unique cities    : \" + cities);\n\n        // Quick answers with Collections helpers\n        List<Integer> groups = log.stream().map(Visit::people).collect(Collectors.toList());\n        System.out.println(\"Biggest group    : \" + Collections.max(groups));\n        System.out.println(\"Groups of 2      : \" + Collections.frequency(groups, 2));\n        System.out.printf(\"Average group    : %.2f%n\", groups.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).average().orElse(0));\n\n        // Busiest city\n        Map<String, Integer> perCity = log.stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Visit::city, TreeMap::new, Collectors.summingInt(Visit::people)));\n        String busiest = Collections.max(perCity.entrySet(), Map.Entry.comparingByValue()).getKey();\n        System.out.println(\"People per city  : \" + perCity);\n        System.out.println(\"Busiest city     : \" + busiest);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Visits from Pune : 4\n// People per day   : {1=6, 2=9, 3=13, 4=2}\n// Unique cities    : [Pune, Mumbai, Nashik, Nagpur]\n// Biggest group    : 7\n// Groups of 2      : 2\n// Average group    : 3.75\n// People per city  : {Mumbai=9, Nagpur=1, Nashik=3, Pune=17}\n// Busiest city     : Pune"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Practice question 3: Trek seat manager with a waiting list</h3>\n<p><strong>Level:</strong> Hard</p>\n<p><strong>Problem:</strong> A trek has only <strong>4 seats</strong>. Booking requests and cancellations arrive as text commands. When a confirmed trekker cancels, the <strong>first person on the waiting list</strong> must get the seat automatically.</p>\n<p><strong>Your task:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Keep two <code>ArrayList&lt;String&gt;</code>: <code>confirmed</code> and <code>waiting</code>.</li>\n<li>Process the commands <code>BOOK name</code> and <code>CANCEL name</code> in order.</li>\n<li>A booking goes to <code>confirmed</code> if a seat is free, otherwise to the end of <code>waiting</code>.</li>\n<li>A cancellation removes the person from whichever list holds them; if a confirmed seat opened up, promote <code>waiting.remove(0)</code>.</li>\n<li>Print a line for every command and the final two lists.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Hints:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>List.remove(Object)</code> returns a <code>boolean</code>: use it to test and remove in one step.</li>\n<li><code>waiting.remove(0)</code> removes by <em>index</em> and returns the element.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Try it on your own first. The program below is one complete solution, with the real output it prints.</em></p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// Practice: seat manager with a waiting list (two ArrayLists)\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class SeatManager {\n    static final int SEATS = 4;\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String[] commands = {\n            \"BOOK Pune\", \"BOOK Mumbai\", \"BOOK Nashik\", \"BOOK Nagpur\", \"BOOK Kolhapur\", \"BOOK Satara\",\n            \"CANCEL Mumbai\", \"CANCEL Satara\", \"BOOK Thane\", \"CANCEL Pune\", \"CANCEL Delhi\"};\n\n        List<String> confirmed = new ArrayList<>();\n        List<String> waiting = new ArrayList<>();\n\n        for (String command : commands) {\n            String[] parts = command.split(\" \");\n            String name = parts[1];\n            if (parts[0].equals(\"BOOK\")) {\n                if (confirmed.size() < SEATS) {\n                    confirmed.add(name);\n                    System.out.println(command + \" -> seat confirmed\");\n                } else {\n                    waiting.add(name);\n                    System.out.println(command + \" -> waiting list position \" + waiting.size());\n                }\n            } else if (confirmed.remove(name)) {                     // remove(Object) returns true if found\n                String message = command + \" -> seat released\";\n                if (!waiting.isEmpty()) {\n                    String promoted = waiting.remove(0);             // first come, first served\n                    confirmed.add(promoted);\n                    message += \", \" + promoted + \" promoted from the waiting list\";\n                }\n                System.out.println(message);\n            } else if (waiting.remove(name)) {\n                System.out.println(command + \" -> removed from the waiting list\");\n            } else {\n                System.out.println(command + \" -> nobody with that name\");\n            }\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Confirmed: \" + confirmed);\n        System.out.println(\"Waiting  : \" + waiting);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// BOOK Pune -> seat confirmed\n// BOOK Mumbai -> seat confirmed\n// BOOK Nashik -> seat confirmed\n// BOOK Nagpur -> seat confirmed\n// BOOK Kolhapur -> waiting list position 1\n// BOOK Satara -> waiting list position 2\n// CANCEL Mumbai -> seat released, Kolhapur promoted from the waiting list\n// CANCEL Satara -> removed from the waiting list\n// BOOK Thane -> waiting list position 1\n// CANCEL Pune -> seat released, Thane promoted from the waiting list\n// CANCEL Delhi -> nobody with that name\n// Confirmed: [Nashik, Nagpur, Kolhapur, Thane]\n// Waiting  : []"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Practice question 4: Second tallest fort and a height filter</h3>\n<p><strong>Level:</strong> Easy</p>\n<p><strong>Problem:</strong> A guide keeps fort heights in an <code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;</code>. Find the <strong>second tallest</strong> height without sorting the original list, and remove every fort lower than 1100 m.</p>\n<p><strong>Your task:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Copy the list and sort the copy in descending order; the second element is the answer.</li>\n<li>Use <code>Collections.max</code> and <code>indexOf</code> to show where the tallest is in the original list.</li>\n<li>Remove all heights below 1100 with <code>removeIf</code> and print the list.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Hints:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A copy keeps the original order untouched.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Try it on your own first. The program below is one complete solution, with the real output it prints.</em></p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// Practice solution: second tallest fort\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class SecondTallestFort {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        List<Integer> heights = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1356, 1403, 1076, 1312, 1450, 1033));\n        List<Integer> copy = new ArrayList<>(heights);               // work on a copy\n        copy.sort(Comparator.reverseOrder());                        // tallest first\n        System.out.println(\"Second tallest: \" + copy.get(1));\n        int top = Collections.max(heights);\n        System.out.println(\"Tallest \" + top + \" is at index \" + heights.indexOf(top) + \" of the original\");\n        heights.removeIf(h -> h < 1100);                             // drop low forts\n        System.out.println(\"After filter  : \" + heights);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Second tallest: 1403\n// Tallest 1450 is at index 4 of the original\n// After filter  : [1356, 1403, 1312, 1450]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Practice question 5: Rotate a trek schedule and remove duplicate stops</h3>\n<p><strong>Level:</strong> Medium</p>\n<p><strong>Problem:</strong> A schedule list has repeated stops. Remove the duplicates but keep the <strong>first</strong> time each stop appears, then rotate the list so it starts from \"Torna\", keeping the circular order.</p>\n<p><strong>Your task:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Walk the list; add a stop to a new list only if it is not there yet.</li>\n<li>Find the index of \"Torna\" and use <code>Collections.rotate</code> to move it to the front.</li>\n<li>Print the list before and after.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Hints:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Collections.rotate(list, -k)</code> moves the first k elements to the end.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Try it on your own first. The program below is one complete solution, with the real output it prints.</em></p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// Practice solution: remove duplicates and rotate\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class RotateSchedule {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        List<String> stops = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"Raigad\", \"Sinhagad\", \"Raigad\", \"Torna\", \"Lohagad\", \"Sinhagad\", \"Rajgad\"));\n        List<String> unique = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (String s : stops) {\n            if (!unique.contains(s)) unique.add(s);                   // keep only the first appearance\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"Unique   : \" + unique);\n        int pos = unique.indexOf(\"Torna\");\n        Collections.rotate(unique, -pos);                            // negative = move left, Torna comes first\n        System.out.println(\"Rotated  : \" + unique);\n        System.out.println(\"Same with LinkedHashSet: \" + new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(stops)));\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Unique   : [Raigad, Sinhagad, Torna, Lohagad, Rajgad]\n// Rotated  : [Torna, Lohagad, Rajgad, Raigad, Sinhagad]\n// Same with LinkedHashSet: [Raigad, Sinhagad, Torna, Lohagad, Rajgad]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Practice question 6: Merge two guides sorted lists and find the median</h3>\n<p><strong>Level:</strong> Hard</p>\n<p><strong>Problem:</strong> Two guides each hold a sorted list of group sizes. Merge them into one sorted list <strong>without using sort</strong>, using two index pointers, then find the median, and use <code>binarySearch</code> to look for a given size.</p>\n<p><strong>Your task:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Use two indexes i and j; always take the smaller current element.</li>\n<li>Copy what remains of the longer list.</li>\n<li>If the size is odd the median is the middle element; if even, the average of the two middle elements.</li>\n<li>Search for 9 and for 10 with <code>Collections.binarySearch</code> and explain the result.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Hints:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Merging two sorted lists is O(n + m), faster than concatenating and sorting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>Try it on your own first. The program below is one complete solution, with the real output it prints.</em></p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// Practice solution: merge two sorted lists, median, binarySearch\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class MergeSortedGuides {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        List<Integer> a = List.of(2, 5, 9, 14);\n        List<Integer> b = List.of(3, 4, 11, 20, 25);\n        List<Integer> merged = new ArrayList<>(a.size() + b.size());   // exact capacity, no regrowth\n        int i = 0, j = 0;\n        while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {                         // two pointers\n            if (a.get(i) <= b.get(j)) merged.add(a.get(i++));\n            else merged.add(b.get(j++));\n        }\n        while (i < a.size()) merged.add(a.get(i++));                   // leftovers\n        while (j < b.size()) merged.add(b.get(j++));\n        System.out.println(\"Merged: \" + merged);\n        int n = merged.size();\n        double median = (n % 2 == 1) ? merged.get(n / 2) : (merged.get(n / 2 - 1) + merged.get(n / 2)) / 2.0;\n        System.out.println(\"Median: \" + median);\n        System.out.println(\"binarySearch 9  -> \" + Collections.binarySearch(merged, 9));\n        int r = Collections.binarySearch(merged, 10);\n        System.out.println(\"binarySearch 10 -> \" + r + \" (not found, would be inserted at \" + (-r - 1) + \")\");\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Merged: [2, 3, 4, 5, 9, 11, 14, 20, 25]\n// Median: 9.0\n// binarySearch 9  -> 4\n// binarySearch 10 -> -6 (not found, would be inserted at 5)"
      ]
    }
